  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-05",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-05",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "xterra treadmill",
      "xterra treadmills",
      "xterra tr95h hiker treadmill",
      "xterra fitness trx1200 folding smart treadmill",
      "xterra tr150 treadmill",
      "xterra tr75 treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Compact Pick: XTERRA TRX1200", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/brands/">Brands</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buyer’s Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">XTERRA Treadmills: A Buyer’s Guide to the Current Range</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        XTERRA Fitness is a budget-to-mid-price treadmill brand owned by Dyaco, the Taiwanese company behind Spirit Fitness, and sold through its own site, Amazon and big-box stores. This guide covers the ten treadmills on XTERRA’s US site, the sale prices, the figures XTERRA itself publishes two different ways, the much-searched TR95H Hiker and TRX1200, what happened to the TR150, and who an XTERRA suits.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 5, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#xterra-trx1200"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41QdXIuP-yL._SL500_.jpg" alt="XTERRA TRX1200" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#xterra-tr75"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51iqxH91NgL._SL500_.jpg" alt="XTERRA TR75" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#sunny-versatile-treadmill"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41DuYz0yB9L._SL500_.jpg" alt="Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Versatile Treadmill" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>XTERRA TRX1200 · XTERRA TR75 · Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Versatile Treadmill</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          XTERRA is one of the names you meet most often when shopping for an inexpensive treadmill:
          on Amazon, at Target and Academy, at Dick's and in warehouse clubs. The range runs from compact
          folders around $500 to a $2,200 "Hiker" with a standing desk, all sold at what XTERRA presents
          as large discounts. The machines are built and backed by Dyaco, a large Taiwanese fitness
          manufacturer that also owns Spirit Fitness. This guide lists every treadmill on XTERRA's US site
          on 5 November 2026 with its price, sorts out which figures the sources agree on, explains why the
          TR150 most reviews mention is no longer on that list, and helps you pick between a compact
          folder, a Sport-series runner and a Hiker.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Compact Pick:</span> <a href="#xterra-trx1200">XTERRA TRX1200</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best for Running:</span> <a href="#xterra-tr75">XTERRA TR75</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#sunny-versatile-treadmill">Sunny Health & Fitness Versatile Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Lowest Price:</span> <a href="#xterra-trx1000">Xterra Fitness TRX1000 Compact Series Treadmill</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          5 November 2026 we checked XTERRA Fitness's US site (its treadmill collection page and the
          TRX1000, TRX1200, TR75 and TR95H product pages), retailer listings at Target, Academy, Dick's
          Sporting Goods and Amazon as they appeared in search results, TreadmillReviewGuru's TR150
          review, Dyaco's own brand and history pages, and the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's
          recall database, which lists no XTERRA or Dyaco recall. Figures on the product cards are those
          on which XTERRA and at least one independent source agree. XTERRA's collection page and its
          TR95H product page give different motor and deck figures for the same machine; we set those
          out as a conflict and keep them off the card. The Sunny Versatile Treadmill card, which stands in
          for the TR95H on Amazon, carries the figures checked for our incline trainer guide. Amazon's own pages would not load for us, so
          Amazon details come from search results and we could not confirm an Amazon price. We have not
          tested any XTERRA treadmill. Prices are XTERRA's sale prices on 5 November 2026, shown against
          higher compare-at prices; they change often. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying
          purchases; that does not change what we say.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Should You Buy an XTERRA Treadmill? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-makes-xterra">Who Makes XTERRA Treadmills?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lineup">The Current XTERRA Treadmill Range</a></li>
            <li><a href="#tr95h-hiker">The TR95H Hiker: What It Is and the Conflicting Figures</a></li>
            <li><a href="#compact-series">The Compact Series: TRX1000, TRX1200, TRX1400 and the TR150</a></li>
            <li><a href="#sport-series">The Sport Series: The XTERRAs for Running</a></li>
            <li><a href="#where-to-buy">Where to Buy an XTERRA, and the Warranty Fine Print</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-should-buy">Which XTERRA Should You Buy?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Should You Buy an XTERRA Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy an XTERRA if you want an inexpensive, compact treadmill for walking and light
            jogging, or a full-length folder for running at about $1,200, and you read the sale price and the
            warranty carefully. Do not buy one expecting the build or the cover of a $2,000 Sole or Horizon.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026 XTERRA's US site listed ten treadmills in four series, from the $499.99 TRX1000
            and WS200 walking treadmill to the $2,199.99 TR95H Hiker, every one shown against a much higher
            compare-at price. The three most searched-for models are easy to place. The <strong>TRX1200</strong> ($549.99) is a compact folder with ten levels of power incline and
            Bluetooth. The <strong>TR95H Hiker</strong> ($2,199.99) is a walking and climbing treadmill with a
            standing desk. The <strong>TR150</strong>, the model most older reviews discuss, was no longer on
            XTERRA's US treadmill page; the current TRX1000 has the same figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            The pattern across the range is consistent: lifetime frame cover, short cover on everything else
            for the cheaper models (90 days on parts and labour for the Compact series), and decks of 16 by 50
            inches on the cheapest machines. Those are walking decks. If you plan to run, the Sport series
            TR75 and TR85, with 20 by 60-inch decks, are the XTERRAs to look at. We have not tested any XTERRA
            treadmill; this guide is built on XTERRA's published figures, retailer listings and independent
            reviews.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-xterra" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Makes XTERRA Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA Fitness is owned by Dyaco International, a Taiwanese fitness equipment manufacturer.
            Dyaco's history page records it becoming a licence partner for the XTERRA name in 2009 and later
            acquiring the brand rights for XTERRA fitness equipment, and its brand page describes XTERRA as a
            Dyaco-owned home fitness line. Dyaco also owns <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Spirit Fitness</a>, which it
            acquired in 2008, and describes itself as the global distributor of <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole Fitness</a>, which it calls a partner
            brand rather than one it owns. The XTERRA name comes from the off-road triathlon series; the
            treadmills are made under Dyaco's rights to the name for fitness equipment, not by the race organiser.
          </p>
          <p>
            That parentage matters for two reasons. First, Dyaco is a large manufacturer with its own US
            support operation, so an XTERRA is not an anonymous marketplace treadmill: the warranty has a
            company behind it, and parts can be ordered. Second, XTERRA sits at the bottom of Dyaco's range.
            Its machines are designed to a price, and the short parts and labour terms on the cheaper models
            reflect that.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the CPSC recall database for XTERRA and for Dyaco on 5 November 2026 and found no
            recall of any XTERRA product. A clean record is a good sign, not a guarantee; register your
            treadmill with XTERRA so it can contact you.
          </p>
