        <div id="tr95h-hiker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The TR95H Hiker: What It Is and the Conflicting Figures</h2>
<p>
            "XTERRA TR95H Hiker treadmill" is one of the most searched XTERRA terms, and the machine is
            different from the rest of the range. It is a walking treadmill with a built-in standing desktop
            and two modes. In XTERRA's running mode, speed runs from 0.5 to 7.5 mph at incline levels 0 to 15;
            in climbing mode, speed is limited to 0.5 to 3.7 mph and incline runs from level 15 to level 40.
            That design — a slow, very steep climbing mode — is aimed at hikers training for elevation and at
            people who want an <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline-trainer</a> style workout, plus a desk for walking while working.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Two motors, two decks</h3>
          <p>
            XTERRA publishes two sets of figures for the TR95H. Its treadmill collection page lists a 3.5 HP
            motor and a 20 by 60-inch running surface. Its TR95H product page, and the Dick's Sporting Goods
            listing as it appeared in search results, give a 3.0 HP motor and a 20 by 52-inch surface. The difference in <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>deck length</a> is
            significant: 52 inches is fine for walking and climbing, short for running. Until XTERRA
            corrects one of its pages, assume the smaller figures, and ask XTERRA's customer service to
            confirm the deck in writing before ordering.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who it suits</h3>
          <p>
            The TR95H suits someone who mainly walks, wants a desk and steep intervals on one machine, and has
            about $2,200 to spend. XTERRA's product page gives a 300 lb maximum user, a 221.6 lb weight and four
            transport wheels that let it turn in place. If you want only a desk treadmill, a dedicated <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk</a> or walking pad
            under your own desk costs far less; our <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk guide</a> covers the set-up. If you want very steep incline with a running deck, see our list of <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills with the highest incline</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cheaper TR75H Hiker ($1,399.99) has a 2.5 HP motor and a 20 by 48-inch deck on XTERRA's page.
            Forty-eight inches is a walking-only length.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compact-series" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Compact Series: TRX1000, TRX1200, TRX1400 and the TR150</h2>
<p>
            The Compact series is where most XTERRA buyers start, and it is where the TR150 used to sit. All
            three current models have a 2.25 HP motor, a 250 lb rating on the models we checked, a 10 mph top
            speed and a folding frame with lift assist.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>TRX1000 ($499.99):</strong> 16 by 50-inch deck, three manual incline levels, 12
            programmes, no Bluetooth, 103.6 lb. XTERRA's warranty is lifetime on the frame, one year on the
            motor and 90 days on the deck, parts and labour.</li>
            <li><strong>TRX1200 ($549.99):</strong> the same deck with ten levels of electronic incline, built-in
            Bluetooth FTMS for <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a> and Kinomap, and the same warranty. For $50 more than the TRX1000, the
            powered incline makes it the better buy for most people.</li>
            <li><strong>TRX1400 ($599.99):</strong> an 18-inch-wide deck, ten incline levels, Bluetooth and
            handlebar speed and incline controls, according to XTERRA's collection page.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What happened to the TR150?</h3>
          <p>
            The TR150 was XTERRA's best-known budget treadmill and still has a large Amazon presence. It was
            not on XTERRA's US treadmill page on 5 November 2026, and we found no XTERRA statement that it has
            been discontinued or replaced. Its figures — 2.25 HP, 16 by 50 inches, 10 mph, three manual incline
            levels, 250 lb, lifetime frame and 90-day parts — are the same as the TRX1000's, and
            TreadmillReviewGuru describes the two as sister models. If you see a TR150 at a lower price than a
            TRX1000, it is a reasonable buy; if the prices are similar, choose the current model, or pay $50
            more for the TRX1200's powered incline.
          </p>
          <p>
            Be careful with Amazon listings. Search results showed more than one XTERRA listing whose title
            mentions the TR150 alongside another model's name, and some listings for "premium folding smart"
            XTERRA treadmills do not name a model number at all. Check the model number in the listing's
            details and on the photos before buying.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The WalkSlim models</h3>
          <p>
            The WS200 and WS300 are <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking treadmills</a> that XTERRA sells fully assembled; the WS300 adds ten
            incline levels. They compete with <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads</a> and are for walking only.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sport-series" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Sport Series: The XTERRAs for Running</h2>
<p>
            If you plan to run, the Sport series is the part of XTERRA's range to look at. The <strong>TR75</strong> ($1,199.99) and <strong>TR85</strong> ($1,499.99) both have 20 by 60-inch
            decks, the length most runners need at speed; the TR65 ($899.99) has a 20 by 55-inch deck that suits
            walkers and joggers better. XTERRA lists the TR75 at 3.25 HP, 0.5 to 12 mph and 350 lb, with lifetime
            frame and motor cover, two years on parts and one year of in-home labour. The TR85 adds a 3.5 HP
            motor and 15 incline levels on XTERRA's page.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those warranty terms are a step up from the Compact series, and the 350 lb rating is unusual at the
            price. At about $1,200 the TR75 competes with entry folders from Horizon and Sole and with the
            better Amazon brands. Our <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill picks</a> and <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">budget treadmill guide</a> compare them; for heavier users, see <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Where to Buy an XTERRA, and the Warranty Fine Print</h2>
<p>
            XTERRA sells through its own US site and widely through retailers. In search results on 5
            November 2026 we saw XTERRA treadmills listed at Amazon, Target, Academy, Dick's Sporting Goods,
            Lowe's, BJ's, QVC and Costco. Prices are close across sellers but not identical: the TRX1200 was
            $549.99 at XTERRA and $599.99 on Academy's listing, and the TR75 between $1,099.99 and $1,299.99.
            Our guides to <a href="/academy-sports-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Academy</a> and <a href="/dicks-sporting-goods-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Dick's</a> treadmills
            and to <a href="/best-treadmills-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills on Amazon</a> cover each seller's returns and delivery.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>XTERRA's site</strong> offered free shipping and a 30-day money-back guarantee on the
            product pages we read. Ask whether return shipping on a 100 to 220 lb treadmill is free.</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty</strong> is XTERRA's whichever seller you use, but registration and proof of
            purchase matter. On the Compact series, 90 days of parts and labour is short; an Amazon reviewer
            summarised in search results described a claim refused after the parts term ended.</li>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> An Amazon reviewer warned that the TR150 needs a grounded outlet; that is
            true of every treadmill, and XTERRA's manuals are the authority for your model.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Read our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> before choosing between a cheap folder with 90-day parts cover and a slightly dearer one with two
            years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which XTERRA Should You Buy?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For walking and light jogging in a small space,</strong> buy the TRX1200. For $50 more than
            the TRX1000 it adds powered incline and Bluetooth. <strong>For running or a heavier user,</strong> buy the TR75, with its 20 by 60-inch deck, 350 lb rating and longer warranty. <strong>For walking at a desk and steep climbing,</strong> the TR95H Hiker, after confirming its
            deck size with XTERRA. <strong>For the lowest price,</strong> a TR150 or TRX1000 at about $500 or
            less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if:</strong> you run fast or often (a 2.25 HP motor and 50-inch deck are not
            built for it); you weigh over 250 lb and are looking at the Compact series; or you want long parts
            and labour cover, which brands like <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole</a> offer at a higher price. For Zwift running, check our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a> before relying on a 10 mph machine.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">
            This guide is about equipment, not health. If you are buying on a doctor's or physical therapist's
            advice, ask them what speed, incline and deck size suit you; nothing here is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an XTERRA Treadmill</h2>
          <p>XTERRA’s range is easy to buy and easy to buy wrongly. These are the commonest mismatches.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a 50-inch deck to run on</h3>
              <p>The Compact and WalkSlim models have 16 by 50-inch decks and 10 mph limits, which suit walking and light jogging. Runners need a longer deck at speed. If you plan to run, start with the Sport series TR75 or TR85, whose decks are 20 by 60 inches.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the compare-at price</h3>
              <p>Every XTERRA on its US site was shown against a compare-at price 40% to 60% higher, with urgency badges. The sale price is the real price, and other retailers charge similar amounts. Compare the sale price with other brands’ actual prices, not with XTERRA’s list price.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering the TR95H without confirming the deck</h3>
              <p>XTERRA’s own pages give the TR95H Hiker a 20 by 52-inch or a 20 by 60-inch running surface, and a 3.0 or 3.5 HP motor. For a walking and climbing machine the difference matters less; for anyone who wants to jog on it, it matters a lot. Ask XTERRA to confirm in writing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the 90-day parts cover</h3>
              <p>The Compact series carries 90 days on parts, deck and labour and one year on the motor. A fault after three months is your bill. If you will use the treadmill daily, the TR75’s two years of parts and lifetime motor cover, or another brand’s longer terms, may be worth the extra cost.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">XTERRA makes inexpensive treadmills with a real manufacturer behind
          them: the TRX1200 is the compact folder to buy at $549.99, with powered incline and Bluetooth,
          and the TR75 is the XTERRA for running, with a 20 by 60-inch deck and a 350 lb rating at
          $1,199.99, on XTERRA's site on 5 November 2026.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the fine print before you buy: 90 days of parts cover on the Compact series, incline stated
          in levels rather than percent, and two different sets of figures for the TR95H Hiker. If you need
          a heavier-duty machine, Dyaco's own <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Spirit</a> range is the
          next step up.
          </p>
